[s0l0!-header][h1]Getters & Setters[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Getters [/b]and [b]Setters [/b]are used to effectively protect your data, particularly when creating classes. For each variable, the [b]get [/b]method returns its value, while the [b]set [/b]method sets the value.

[b]Getters [/b]start with [b]get[/b], followed by the variable name, with the first letter of the variable name capitalized.
[b]Setters [/b]start with [b]set[/b], followed by the variable name, with the first letter of the variable name capitalized.

[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="java" ]public class Vehicle {
  private String color;

  // Getter
  public String [b]getColor[/b]() {
    return color;
  }

 // Setter
  public void [b]setColor[/b](String c) {
    this.color = c;
  }
}[/code]
The [b]getter [/b]method returns the value of the attribute.
The [b]setter [/b]method takes a parameter and assigns it to the attribute.
[note]The keyword [b]this [/b]is used to refer to the current object. Basically, [b]this.color[/b] is the [b]color [/b]attribute of the current object.[/note]

